# fff - Fairly Fast Fetcher

> [!NOTE]
> This document was written by AI.

Requests a list of URLs provided on `stdin` concurrently and writes headers and response bodies to disk.

The main idea is to launch requests with controlled concurrency and delay without waiting for slow responses to block execution.

---

## Installation

```bash
cd fff
go build -o fff.exe .
```

---

## Features

- **High-Speed Concurrency**: Adjustable concurrency (`-c`) and delay (`-d`) to maximize throughput while respecting rate limits.
- **Smart JS Beautification (`--js-beautify`)**: Automatically detects JavaScript responses (via URL path, file extension, or `Content-Type: application/javascript`) and reformats minified/bundled code in-place using `js-beautify`.
- **Organized Storage Hierarchy**: Saves responses to `<output>/<domain>/<path>/<file>-body` and `<file>-headers`.
- **Filtering Options**: Filter by HTTP status code (`-s`), match body contents (`-M`), skip HTML (`--ignore-html`), or ignore empty responses (`--ignore-empty`).

---

## Usage

### Basic Fetch & Save All
```bash
cat urls.txt | fff -S -o out
```

### Save Only 200 OK Responses & Auto-Beautify JavaScript
```bash
cat js_endpoints.txt | fff -s 200 --js-beautify -o out
```

### Custom Concurrency, Headers & Proxy
```bash
cat urls.txt | fff -s 200 -c 50 -d 50 -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" -x http://127.0.0.1:8080 -o out
```

---

## CLI Options

```text
Options:
  -b, --body <data>         Request body
  -c, --concurrency <num>   Maximum concurrent requests (default: 20, 0 for unlimited)
  -d, --delay <delay>       Delay between issuing requests (ms) (default: 100)
  -H, --header <header>     Add a header to the request (can be specified multiple times)
      --header-only         Save only response headers, skip saving body
      --ignore-html         Don't save HTML files; useful when looking for non-HTML files only
      --ignore-empty        Don't save empty files
      --js-beautify         Format and beautify saved JavaScript files using js-beautify
  -k, --keep-alive          Use HTTP Keep-Alive
  -m, --method              HTTP method to use (default: GET, or POST if body is specified)
  -M, --match <string>      Save responses that include <string> in the body
  -o, --output <dir>        Directory to save responses in (will be created, default: out)
  -s, --save-status <code>  Save responses with given status code (can be specified multiple times)
  -S, --save                Save all responses
  -t, --timeout <time>      HTTP request timeout (e.g. 10s, 5000ms, or seconds, default: 10s)
  -x, --proxy <proxyURL>    Use the provided HTTP proxy
```

---

## Tuning

On Unix systems with heavy concurrency, you may want to increase your open file descriptor limit:

```bash
ulimit -n 16384
```
